  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. Along x, steps overshoot when the learning rate is above 1/3 and swings grow when it is above 2/3. Along z, a step never overshoots for learning rates up to 1.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with mean 0 and size 2·√((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch size B, so it is zero at 64. On average a step goes downhill, but a single noisy step can raise the loss.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 2 times their true step length. The orange one is the actual mini-batch step and the grey one is the full-data step, −learning rate × true slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished). It ends earlier as diverged when the loss goes above 6 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Past the rim the ball is drawn at the rim, and the readout keeps the true loss.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed, run number and step number. Reset replays run 1. 'New run, keep last path' uses the next run's noise and keeps the last path as the ghost."
  ]
}